{"author":{"id":"6af04b5756f10b3005a23338b8f25a6c1d7564fe1ddddc77e70806b2f14ac31a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789490175,"nonce":"ncqqEb6s0l0q957GG8VrSQtvqeA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOUKdEis8AAAABRbk63A","github-url":"https://github.com/ff6347/gic-lang/issues/12","origin":"github"},"title":"Run GIC headlessly and inspect drawing commands","message":"## Parent\n\ngit-bug issue `60b2077` — PRD: GIC v0.9 workshop environment\n\n## What to build\n\nProvide `gic run \u003cfile\u003e` as headless execution through the same parse, analysis, and interpreter pipeline as the IDE. The default terminal output contains GIC `print` output; `--show-commands` additionally exposes the recorded drawing commands. Image generation and display are deferred so a future shell renderer, potentially using Skia, can be designed separately.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A valid program executes through the shared core and exits successfully.\n- [ ] Default stdout contains ordered GIC `print` output and does not dump the AST or drawing commands.\n- [ ] `--show-commands` adds a documented, stable serialization of the ordered drawing-command list.\n- [ ] Output emitted before a runtime failure is preserved before the source-located diagnostic and unsuccessful exit.\n- [ ] Parse, analysis, runtime, usage, and file failures follow the same diagnostic and exit-status conventions as `gic check`.\n- [ ] The command creates no image, browser window, desktop window, or server.\n- [ ] Process-level tests cover default output, command inspection, failures, and option validation.\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- git-bug issue `a39002a` — Ship gic check","files":null}]}